## 1. Result

The regular-locus theorem
`q79_IntrinsicSpectralStrainQuotient_and_ShortedHessian_Theorem_v1`
left one precise seam: an off-diagonal spectral edge could vanish, causing the
Jacobian of

\[
 \Phi(X)=
 (d_0,d_1,d_2,|z_{12}|^2,|z_{20}|^2,|z_{01}|^2)
 \tag{1}
\]

to lose rank. This theorem closes that seam without pretending that a
singular quotient is a smooth six-dimensional vector space.

The exact conclusions are:

1. the image of (1) is the support-stratified cone
   \(\mathbb R_D^3\times\mathbb R_{\geq0,E}^3\), with eight strata;
2. on the stratum with edge support \(S\), \(\operatorname{rank}D\Phi=3+|S|\);
3. every absent edge has zero first jet and an exact nonnegative quadratic
   normal jet \(|v_e|^2\);
4. the reduced-Green shorted Hessian remains exact on each stratum's linear
   tangent;
5. at a zero edge, positive quadratic source cost descends with degree one in
   the target normal strain, so no finite ordinary six-dimensional target
   Hessian can represent it;
6. a surjective rank-six tangent map, or a local \(C_4\) lift whose derivative
   is \(J_{DE}(d,e)=(-e,d)\), forces all three edges to be nonzero.

Thus the physical q79 branch does not need an independent assumption saying
that the three edge amplitudes are nonzero. The rank-six/quarter-turn contract
already forces the selected endpoint into the regular top stratum. The actual
selected endpoint, Green operator and same-source symmetry lift remain open.

## 2. Setup

Write a Hermitian spectral endomorphism as

\[
 X=(d_0,d_1,d_2;z_0,z_1,z_2),
 \qquad (z_0,z_1,z_2)=(z_{12},z_{20},z_{01}).
\]

For \(S\subseteq\{0,1,2\}\), define

\[
 \mathcal X_S=
 \{X:z_e\neq0\text{ exactly when }e\in S\},
\]

and

\[
 \mathcal Y_S=
 \mathbb R^3_D\times
 (0,\infty)^S\times\{0\}^{S^c}.
 \tag{2}
\]

The support mask is only a stratum label. It is not a physical selector or a
new branch parameter.

## 3. Global Image and Strata

### Theorem 3.1

The exact image of \(\Phi\) is

\[
 \mathcal Y=\mathbb R^3_D\times[0,\infty)^3_E
             =\coprod_{S\subseteq\{0,1,2\}}\mathcal Y_S.
 \tag{3}
\]

The closure order is

\[
 \overline{\mathcal Y_S}
 =\coprod_{T\subseteq S}\mathcal Y_T.
 \tag{4}
\]

The sheet group \(S_3\) permutes these strata. Its stratum orbits are the four
support-cardinality classes, with multiplicities

\[
 1,3,3,1.
 \tag{5}
\]

### Proof

Every edge norm is nonnegative, so the image is contained in (3). Conversely,
given \(r_e\geq0\), choose \(z_e=\sqrt{r_e}\). The diagonal coordinates are
unrestricted. This proves (3). Equations (4) and (5) follow by allowing any
chosen subset of positive edge norms to tend to zero and by permuting the
three edge labels. No general singular-quotient theorem is needed for this
finite calculation. QED.

## 4. First and Second Jets

For a source tangent

\[
 V=(\delta d_0,\delta d_1,\delta d_2;v_0,v_1,v_2),
\]

the differential is

\[
 D\Phi_X(V)
 =\left(\delta d_e,2\operatorname{Re}(\overline z_e v_e)\right)_{e=0}^2.
 \tag{6}
\]

Because (1) is quadratic, there is an exact identity with no remainder:

\[
 \Phi(X+tV)
 =\Phi(X)+tD\Phi_X(V)+t^2Q(V),
 \qquad Q_e(V)=|v_e|^2.
 \tag{7}
\]

### Theorem 4.1

On \(\mathcal X_S\),

\[
 \operatorname{rank}D\Phi_X=3+|S|,
 \qquad
 \dim\ker D\Phi_X=6-|S|.
 \tag{8}
\]

For \(e\notin S\), the first edge-normal row vanishes, but the parabolic
normal coefficient is

\[
 Q_e(V)=|v_e|^2\in[0,\infty).
 \tag{9}
\]

Every value in the ray in (9) occurs. Hence the lawful local data are a linear
tangent together with a quadratic normal cone, not a rank-deficient linear
map masquerading as a six-dimensional submersion.

### Proof

Each diagonal contributes one independent row. If \(z_e\neq0\), the real
linear functional \(v_e\mapsto2\operatorname{Re}(\overline z_ev_e)\) has rank
one. If \(z_e=0\), it has rank zero. This proves (8). Equation (7) proves (9),
and choosing \(v_e=(\sqrt\eta,0)\) realizes any \(\eta\geq0\). QED.

The tangent cone of (3) at a point of support \(S\) is

\[
 C_S=\mathbb R_D^3\times
     \mathbb R_E^S\times
     \mathbb R_{\geq0,E}^{S^c},
 \tag{10}
\]

with lineality space

\[
 L_S=\mathbb R_D^3\oplus\mathbb R_E^S.
 \tag{11}
\]

The full relative-phase invariant relation from the predecessor theorem is

\[
 u^2+v^2=r_0r_1r_2.
 \tag{12}
\]

If any edge vanishes, (12) forces \(u=v=0\). Thus the triangle phase survives
only on the top stratum \(S=\{0,1,2\}\). On every boundary stratum the strain
coordinates contain the complete continuous relative-phase quotient data.

For a smooth target function \(F\), the exact second-order chain rule is

\[
 D^2(F\circ\Phi)_X[V,W]
 =D^2F_{\Phi(X)}[D\Phi_XV,D\Phi_XW]
  +DF_{\Phi(X)}[D^2\Phi_X(V,W)].
 \tag{13}
\]

The second term in (13) cannot be dropped at a zero edge.

## 5. Stratumwise Shorting

Let \(H_Q\) be a positive source Hessian on the reduced source domain and let
\(G_Q=H_Q^{-1}\). Let \(J_S\) be (6) with codomain restricted to the linear
tangent of \(\mathcal Y_S\).

### Theorem 5.1

The covariance

\[
 C_S=J_SG_QJ_S^*
 \tag{14}
\]

is positive and invertible on the \((3+|S|)\)-dimensional stratum tangent. Its
inverse

\[
 H_S=(J_SG_QJ_S^*)^{-1}
 \tag{15}
\]

is the exact constrained tangential Hessian.

### Proof

Equation (8) says that \(J_S\) has full row rank. For nonzero \(y\),

\[
 \langle y,C_Sy\rangle
 =\langle J_S^*y,G_QJ_S^*y\rangle>0,
\]

because \(J_S^*y\neq0\) and \(G_Q>0\). The standard constrained quadratic
minimization then gives (15). QED.

For \(|S|<3\), the full six-row covariance \(D\Phi_XG_QD\Phi_X^*\) has rank
\(3+|S|\). A Moore-Penrose inverse can describe (15) on the linear image, but
it cannot manufacture the missing normal directions in (9).

## 6. Boundary-Hessian No-Go and Repair

For a pure boundary-normal displacement \(\eta\in[0,\infty)^{S^c}\), define

\[
 \mathcal Q_{H,S}(\eta)=
 \inf\left\{
   \frac12\langle V,H_QV\rangle:
   J_SV=0,
   (|v_e|^2)_{e\notin S}=\eta
 \right\}.
 \tag{16}
\]

The feasible set is nonempty. Positivity and finite dimensionality make the
infimum positive for \(\eta\neq0\) and attained.

### Theorem 6.1

For every \(c\geq0\),

\[
 \mathcal Q_{H,S}(c\eta)=c\mathcal Q_{H,S}(\eta).
 \tag{17}
\]

Consequently, at a zero edge there is no finite ordinary target Hessian
\(K\) whose quadratic form represents the descended source cost in all normal
directions.

### Proof

The substitution \(V\mapsto\sqrt cV\) maps the feasible set for \(\eta\)
bijectively to the feasible set for \(c\eta\). The source quadratic form is
multiplied by \(c\), proving (17).

An ordinary target quadratic would instead satisfy

\[
 \frac12\langle c\eta,K(c\eta)\rangle
 =c^2\frac12\langle\eta,K\eta\rangle.
\]

This cannot equal the positive degree-one function (17) for all \(c\). QED.

For the unit isotropic witness, a source path \(z_e(t)=t\) gives

\[
 \delta r_e=t^2,
 \qquad
 \Delta S=\frac12t^2=\frac12\delta r_e.
 \tag{18}
\]

The correct global object is therefore:

```text
linear tangent directions  -> reduced-Green shorted Hessian (15)
zero-edge normal directions -> degree-one parabolic value function (16)
```

It is not one ordinary six-dimensional Hessian at the boundary.

## 7. Rank-Six and C4 Regularity Forcing

### Theorem 7.1

A surjective linearized map from the source to the six-dimensional strain
carrier exists only on the top support stratum.

### Proof

By (8), \(\operatorname{rank}D\Phi_X=6\) if and only if \(|S|=3\). QED.

### Theorem 7.2

Let

\[
 J_{DE}(d,e)=(-e,d).
 \tag{19}
\]

No boundary tangent cone \(C_S\), \(|S|<3\), can be mapped by \(J_{DE}\) to
the tangent cone of any support stratum. Therefore a local \(C_4\) action by
stratified diffeomorphisms whose derivative is (19) forces the endpoint into
the top stratum.

### Proof

The lineality space of \(C_S\) is (11). Under (19),

\[
 J_{DE}L_S=\mathbb R_D^S\oplus\mathbb R_E^3.
 \tag{20}
\]

Every support cone \(C_T\) has lineality

\[
 L_T=\mathbb R_D^3\oplus\mathbb R_E^T.
 \tag{21}
\]

Equations (20) and (21) can be equal only when \(S=T=\{0,1,2\}\). The
derivative of a local stratified diffeomorphism must carry tangent cones and
their lineality spaces isomorphically. Hence no boundary stratum admits the
required derivative. QED.

This does not assert that \(J_{DE}\) is a global automorphism of the orthant.
It is precisely a tangent symmetry on the regular top stratum, as required by
the predecessor theorem.

